// PEST CONTROL, SOLO (2026-10-12). Every number here, and where it came from.
//
// WHY SOLO AND NOT THE REAL THING. Old School needs five players in the lander before a game
// starts. On a server this size the lander never fills, so the faithful version is content nobody
// ever sees - and it is also the harder build, because five players need a lobby, a queue and a
// per-player activity score. A solo variant has none of that: one player, one instance, and the
// pressure comes from the clock instead of from other people being useless.
//
// WHAT IS NOT BUILT, so it is not hunted for: the three landers by combat level (one lander here,
// and the pests' tier comes off your combat level instead), the barricades the ravagers exist to
// chew, the spinners' portal-repairing, and the per-player activity requirement. Those are
// multiplayer mechanics or they need the barricade objects, and none of them is load-bearing for
// one player against a clock.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The island
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// m41_40 is the pest island, all 64x64 of it, copied whole into an instance - 8x8 zones, the same
// loop the Fight Cave uses (minigames/game_fightcave/scripts/fightcave.rs2). It was one of the
// three fully isolated map squares on the server: nothing but a script could ever put a player on
// it, which is why nothing ever had.
^pest_solo_template = 0_41_40_0_0
^pest_solo_zones = 8

// WHERE EVERYTHING STANDS (corrected 13 October, reported from play: "portals are all in the wrong
// locations ... void knight in wrong location"). They were, and the reason is worth keeping.
//
// THE FIRST VERSION MEASURED THE ISLAND AND IGNORED IT. It asked the collision map which tiles have
// a clear 3x3, then spread four portals over them by greedy farthest-point and put the knight at
// the centroid. Every one of those answers was true - clear, reachable, well separated - and all
// five were in the wrong place, because "a tile that fits" is not "the tile this island was drawn
// around". It is the same mistake as the diary teleports: a measurement that answers a question
// nobody asked.
//
// THE ISLAND ITSELF SAYS WHERE THEY GO, and it says it twice. The ground is painted in four small
// patches of their own underlay, one per portal - and standing on each patch is a FRAME of vine
// locs, a 3x3 ring with an arm out of two opposite sides, named for the site it belongs to:
//
//   patch underlay 121  abyssal_ground_dark   frame pest_vine_*_range    ring x 4-6  z 31-33  WEST
//   patch underlay 142  fairy_grass           frame pest_vine_*_master   ring x21-23 z  9-11  SOUTH-WEST
//   patch underlay 61   desert2               frame pest_vine_*_magic    ring x45-47 z 10-12  SOUTH-EAST
//   patch underlay 59   greenice              frame pest_vine_*_melee    ring x56-58 z 28-30  EAST
//
// The frame is the better of the two, and the one these coordinates come from: a patch is nine
// tiles across and only says roughly where, while the ring is exactly the 3x3 a size-3 portal
// fills. Nothing had to be invented for either.
//
// AND THE COORDINATE IS THE RING'S SOUTH-WEST TILE, not its centre (corrected 13 October, reported
// from play: "they aren't sitting directly in their frame"). npc_add places an npc by its
// south-west corner and a portal is 3x3, so it occupies coord..coord+2 - putting a portal on the
// ring's CENTRE draws it one tile north and one tile east of the ring it is supposed to fill.
// That is the whole of that fault, and it was uniform across all four.
//
// WHICH PORTAL ON WHICH PATCH is half-settled by the cache and half not. pest_portal_1_active's
// own animation is pest_portal_on_anim_EAST and pest_portal_2_active's is _WEST, so those two place
// themselves. Portals 3 and 4 share the unsuffixed pest_portal_on_anim and the cache says nothing,
// so they take the two southern patches west-to-east in id order - 3 south-west, 4 south-east.
// THAT PAIR IS THE ONE GUESS HERE: if their colours look swapped in game, swap these two lines and
// nothing else.
//
// NO TRAILING COMMENTS ON THESE FOUR LINES. A constant is textual substitution, and these are
// substituted into enum VALUES (configs/pest_solo.enum) rather than into script - so a trailing
// "// ..." is carried into the value and the packer rejects the row. The coordinate each one
// encodes is in the table above.
//
// east, the melee frame.  ring x56-58 z28-30, so the south-west tile is 56,28 = (2680,2588)
^pest_solo_portal_e = 0_41_40_56_28
// west, the range frame.  ring x4-6 z31-33, so 4,31 = (2628,2591)
^pest_solo_portal_w = 0_41_40_4_31
// south-west, the master frame.  ring x21-23 z9-11, so 21,9 = (2645,2569)
^pest_solo_portal_sw = 0_41_40_21_9
// south-east, the magic frame.  ring x45-47 z10-12, so 45,10 = (2669,2570)
^pest_solo_portal_se = 0_41_40_45_10
^pest_solo_portals = 4

// THE VOID KNIGHT IS OLD SCHOOL'S OWN COORDINATE, (2656,2592), off the wiki's LocLine row for
// Void Knight (Pest Control) through tools/models/osrsnpcspawns.py - the same scraper the God Wars
// spawns came from. It lands him in the middle of his own building, which the collision map
// confirms: the 11x11 around it is walls on every side with a doorway east and west. The measured
// guess had him ten tiles south of it, standing outside his own base.
^pest_solo_knight_coord = 0_41_40_32_32

// Outside the knight's building and south of it, so you arrive looking at the thing you have to
// keep alive rather than standing in the room with it.
^pest_solo_landing = 0_41_40_35_19

// And where leaving puts you back - the outpost pier, beside the lander squire.
^pest_solo_outside = 0_41_41_36_52

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The clock, and the only way to lose
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// EIGHT MINUTES, not Old School's five, and the extra three are what make a solo game winnable by
// the people it is offered to.
//
// Measured rather than guessed (engine tools/sim/_ttk.ts, five runs per loadout, medians):
//
//   maxed + abyssal whip        52 ticks a portal   208 for four
//   maxed + rune scimitar       54                  216
//   level 70s + rune scimitar   84                  336
//   combat 40 + mithril scim   132                  528
//
// Against a 500-tick clock the bottom row is already over before a single step is walked, and the
// portals are 37 tiles apart. At 800 even the combat-40 player who the lander's own requirement
// lets aboard has room to finish - which is the point, because offering somebody a game they
// cannot win is worse than not offering it. Raising the requirement instead was the alternative
// and it is the wrong trade: it would make this high-level content to save three minutes.
^pest_solo_ticks = 800

// THE VOID KNIGHT'S HITPOINTS ARE OLD SCHOOL'S - 200, from the OSRS cache (npc 2950). What is NOT
// Old School is how he loses them.
//
// THE PESTS CANNOT ATTACK HIM, because nothing in this engine lets an npc attack an npc: combat is
// always against a player uid (skill_combat/scripts/npc/npc_combat.rs2 reads
// %npc_aggressive_player). So the knight is not an npc in a fight, he is a clock that runs faster
// the more portals are open - one hitpoint per living portal every ^pest_solo_drain_interval ticks.
//
// The numbers are chosen so that DOING NOTHING LOSES, and loses before the timer: four portals for
// the full 800 ticks drains 4 * 800 / 12 = 266 against his 200, so he dies around tick 600.
// Killing portals is not optional and killing them early is worth more than killing them well.
//
// The interval went 8 -> 12 with the clock's 500 -> 800: at 8 the knight would have died on tick
// 400 of an 800-tick game, which is the clock doing nothing for its second half. tools/sim/
// pestsolo.ts asserts the tick the knight actually falls on, so this arithmetic is checked rather
// than asserted.
^pest_solo_knight_hp = 200
^pest_solo_drain_interval = 12

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The pests
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Each living portal keeps this many alive at once, respawning as they die. Old School's portals
// pour out far more than this, for twenty-five players; two per portal is eight at once for one.
^pest_solo_per_portal = 2

// How long a spawned pest lives if nothing kills it. NOT ZERO - the engine's DurationValid rejects
// anything below 1, so npc_add(coord, type, 0) compiles and then throws at runtime. Five hours is
// past any game, so in practice they live until they die or the instance goes with them; the number
// exists so a leaked instance eventually empties itself.
^pest_solo_npc_life = 30000

// WHICH TIER YOU FIGHT, off your combat level. Old School splits this across three landers (novice
// 40+, intermediate 70+, veteran 100+); there is one lander here, so the lander asks your combat
// level the way the landers would have.
^pest_solo_combat_req = 40
^pest_solo_tier_mid = 70
^pest_solo_tier_high = 100

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Commendation points
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Old School pays 3-4 points for a win and nothing for a loss, and the Void Knights' shop prices
// are in the hundreds, so a piece of void equipment is dozens of games either way. Those prices are
// kept (pest_rewards.rs2) rather than softened, because the grind is the same grind and inventing
// a cheaper one would make the gear mean less than it does in the game it came from.
^pest_solo_points_win = 3
// One more if the knight finishes above half, which is the only thing in the game that rewards
// killing portals FAST rather than merely killing them.
^pest_solo_points_bonus = 1
^pest_solo_bonus_threshold = 100
